Yamlover Projectional Editor — the state machine
description: |
    Entries of this document are the STATES of the unlocked yamlover editor (the cell
    tree). Each state is an omni node - the self-value is its TITLE, `description`
    says what is drawn on screen and where the focus/caret sits. Every other keyed
    entry is a TRANSITION - the key names the key press (or event), the value points
    at the next state. A CONDITIONAL transition nests condition-keyed pointers instead
    of a single pointer. The code these states project onto — the yed machine
    (tools/yed/src: the dispatch grammar, the pure apply layer, the cell registry) —
    is documented in EDITOR.md.

    Conventions.
    `alphanumeric` covers letters, digits and any ordinary character that starts or
    extends a plain token — QUOTES INCLUDED: yed projects no quote glyphs and has no
    quote mode; a `"` is a character of the token, an authored-quoted token commits
    with its spelling kept (raw-first) and re-opens as its authored text. The keyed
    trigger mirrors how
    one types yamlover SOURCE - `key_colon_space` (a word, the colon, a space) puts
    the value INLINE on the same row, while `key_colon_enter` (the colon confirmed by
    Enter) puts the value on the NEXT rows - the entry's value becomes a nested block
    with an indented entry hole. In VALUE position the same triggers open a nested
    structure COMPACTLY - `- name: `, `- - ` and their kin keep editing on the SAME
    row (the first nested child rides the marker line, exactly like compact YAML);
    only Enter breaks to the next row. A `key_colon` whose key ALREADY exists in the
    node is rejected with the error ring - keys are unique per node. Backspace in an
    empty value cell UNDOES the last structural token (the colon or the dash), never
    the whole entry. Clicking any cell with the mouse enters that cell's focused
    state. A committed KEY is itself an editable cell - the caret ENTERS it (a click
    or an arrow walk) and editing its text RENAMES the key on commit; the server
    routes the rename by STORAGE (a directory-backed member is moved on disk, an
    inline keyed entry has its key token rewritten - THE CONCRETE IS NOT A STATE),
    and a key duplicating a sibling is rejected with the error ring (see
    key_cell_editing). Commits never lose focus - every transition that persists a value ALSO
    names the cell the caret lands in; behavior is guided by the state, never by
    leftovers of the previous one.

    THE PROJECTION RULE. One machine, several PROJECTIONS. The pure yed state
    (tools/yed/src/state.ts: the parser IR document + the cursor) is what edits;
    a projection only DRAWS. The source projection (tools/yed/src/cells.tsx,
    mounted by the server's yed-editor.tsx) draws rows of yamlover token cells
    and applies the typing grammar below. The chapter projection
    (tools/yed/src/chapter/) draws its own cell zoo over the same IR and does NOT
    use the typing grammar at all - no holes, no `- `/`key:` decisions. The
    states in this table are the MACHINE's; a `chapter_*` state names the same
    machine state seen under the chapter projection.

    THE FORMAT RULE. A block's format is its `!!<…$defs:X>` tag - table, bullets,
    numbered - and NORMAL CHAPTER is the untagged case, so choosing it DROPS the
    tag rather than stamping one (a task narrows its body to subtasks; a chapter
    tag inside one would assert something false). An untagged container INHERITS
    from its enclosing format, compounding down the whole chain: a sublist stays
    that list's kind at any depth, a table is exactly two levels (rows, then
    cells) and anything below a cell is a chapter again. The format is READ from
    the spine on demand, never stored - so there is no "current format" to fall
    out of step with the caret.

    THE LEVEL RULE (unified). Enter after committing a value DESCENDS - the entry
    hole it opens lives INSIDE the just-committed node: the value becomes the
    node's own scalar line (drawn on the marker's row) and the node is now an omni
    whose body you are editing, one level deeper. The hole opens on the row RIGHT
    AFTER the value line - at the omni's `selfAt` among its fields - never past
    fields that already sit below the line (Enter at a line's end means "a new row
    below THIS row" in every editor). Editing STAYS at that level until
    Shift-Tab climbs back out; consecutive same-level entries therefore cost a
    Shift-Tab each. The rule is the same at every depth, the document root
    included - `12` + Enter grows the document's body exactly as `- scalar` + Enter
    grows that entry's. The one exception - a committed REFERENCE holds no
    children, so its Enter opens the next SIBLING hole.

    THE HEAD-OF-ROW EXCEPTION to THE LEVEL RULE. Enter with the caret at the very
    START of a committed value cell (before its first character) does not descend:
    the row is pushed DOWN and a fresh SIBLING entry hole opens BEFORE the entry,
    caret in it - the gesture every text editor gives Enter at a line's head. Only
    an ENTRY's row has a row above it - at the document root, and mid-text or at
    the end of the cell, the level rule stands.

    THE PROSE EXCEPTION to THE LEVEL RULE. Under the CHAPTER projection Enter makes
    a SIBLING block, not a descent, and TAB is the only descent. Prose is a stream
    of paragraphs, not a tree of typed values: a writer pressing Enter means "next
    paragraph", and every prose editor in the world agrees. In a CHAPTER, Tab acts
    on the CURRENT block: a chunk becomes a subchapter title (and Shift-Tab unmakes
    one - exact inverses, zero ops, since `- A` is the same line either way); it
    never conscripts the previous chunk into being a title. In a LIST, Tab keeps
    the classic outliner move - the item nests under the previous item as a sublist
    of the same kind - because a list item's identity IS its position. Inside a
    TABLE, Tab is not a move at all but a caret walk across the grid: next cell,
    wrapping to the next row at a row's end, appending a row at the very last cell.

    THE REPRESENTATION RULE. The concrete stores the REPRESENTATION and the
    renderer REPRODUCES it - never re-derives it from the decoded value. A quoted
    scalar keeps its quotes, a hex int its spelling, and a block scalar its AUTHORED
    header (`|`, `|-`, `|+`, `>`, ...) with its content lines exactly as they sit in
    the source - no derived trailing blank line, no silently swapped chomping.
    Editing keeps the representation too: committing a block cell re-emits the SAME
    header over the edited lines (a fresh block typed via `|` is authored `|`), and
    only a text that cannot live under a block header at all falls back to a quoted
    scalar. The rule holds wherever a scalar is drawn - a value cell, an omni
    SELF-VALUE line, the document root. The self-value line's POSITION among the
    node's entries is representation too - the line is SAVED at the position it
    was typed (after the entries that precede it), never hoisted to the top.

    THE CONCRETE IS NOT A STATE. The editor edits ANY yamlover-shaped node the same
    way - a file document, a directory, a directory-backed document. WHERE a newly
    typed child is STORED (the parent directory's `.yo/body.yo` overlay
    vs a nested real directory) is decided server-side by the concrete-derivation
    module (derive-concrete.ts: a keyed CONTAINER child becomes a directory,
    everything else lands in the body) - invisible to the editing states.

    Pseudo-states (`nop`, `error_flash`, `focus_prev`, `focus_next`) stand for
    "consume the event and stay", "flash the red ring and stay", and the two
    focus-walk actions whose next real state is the adjacent cell's focused state.
    Tab / Shift-Tab MOVE the focused entry and the FOCUS FOLLOWS it - the caret
    stays in the same cell at its new depth, ready for the next keystroke. The
    `open_brace` / `open_bracket` transitions apply IDENTICALLY at the document
    root, in entry holes and in value holes - `{` always opens brace-style (flow)
    mapping cells, `[` bracket-style sequence cells, with the closer projected and
    the first inner cell holding the caret.

nop: Nothing happens
    description: A pseudo-state - the event is consumed, the current state stays

error_flash: Rejected input
    description: A pseudo-state - the cell flashes the error ring, text kept, the previous state stays

focus_prev: Focus the previous cell
    description: A pseudo-state - the caret lands at the END of the preceding cell in visual order; continue in that cell's focused state

focus_next: Focus the next cell
    description: A pseudo-state - the caret lands at the START of the following cell in visual order; continue in that cell's focused state

locked: Editor locked
    description: Pending edits are flushed and the read-only view returns. Esc reaches here from any state - the first press cancels the cell being edited (text reverts), the second locks

empty: Empty document
    description: The one root hole drawn with the cursor inside; no content exists yet
    alphanumeric: *: unquoted_scalar_appending
    asterisk: *: pointer_entry
    pipe: *: block_scalar_editing
    dash_space: *: value_hole
    key_colon_space: *: value_hole
    key_colon_enter: *: entry_hole
    bang_bang_lt: *: meta_tag_editing
    open_brace: *: flow_map_editing
    open_bracket: *: flow_seq_editing
    enter: *: nop
    backspace: *: nop
    tab: *: nop
    shift_tab: *: nop
    left_arrow: *: nop
    right_arrow: *: nop
    up_arrow: *: nop
    down_arrow: *: nop

unquoted_scalar_appending: Plain token editing, caret at the end
    description: A non-empty cell with the part of the plain scalar entered so far, cursor after the last character. The keyed trigger is LIVE here exactly as in a hole - the moment the text reads `key: ` the cell restructures, whether it grew from a hole or is a COMMITTED token being re-edited (`abc` + `:` + space behaves the same as typing `a b c : space` from scratch)
    alphanumeric: *: unquoted_scalar_appending
    key_colon_space:
        "the key is free in the node": *: value_hole
        "the key already exists in the node": *: error_flash
    left_arrow: *: unquoted_scalar_inserting
    right_arrow: *: nop
    backspace:
        "more than one character": *: unquoted_scalar_appending
        "the only character removed, cell is a root hole or entry hole": *: empty_cell_of_origin
    enter:
        "the token is not a valid scalar": *: error_flash
        "a BARE token in an entry hole, but the node already has its scalar line": *: error_flash
        "valid": *: entry_hole
    tab:
        "the cell is an entry with a previous ordinal sibling": *: unquoted_scalar_appending
        "otherwise": *: nop
    shift_tab:
        "the cell is an entry below the root level": *: unquoted_scalar_appending
        "otherwise": *: nop
    up_arrow: *: focus_prev
    down_arrow: *: focus_next

unquoted_scalar_inserting: Plain token editing, caret inside
    description: A non-empty cell with part of the scalar entered so far, cursor at the beginning or in the middle. The keyed trigger is LIVE here too - an edit that makes the text read `key: ...` restructures the cell wherever the caret sits
    alphanumeric:
        "the text now reads `key: ` and the key is free in the node": *: value_hole
        "caret reached the end": *: unquoted_scalar_appending
        "otherwise": *: unquoted_scalar_inserting
    left_arrow:
        "was at the beginning": *: nop
        "otherwise": *: unquoted_scalar_inserting
    right_arrow:
        "reached the end": *: unquoted_scalar_appending
        "otherwise": *: unquoted_scalar_inserting
    backspace:
        "was at the beginning": *: focus_prev
        "otherwise": *: unquoted_scalar_inserting
    enter:
        "caret at the very beginning of a COMMITTED entry's value - the row is pushed down, a fresh sibling entry hole opens BEFORE this row (THE HEAD-OF-ROW EXCEPTION; at the root the level rule stands)": *: entry_hole
        "the token is not a valid scalar": *: error_flash
        "a BARE token in an entry hole, but the node already has its scalar line": *: error_flash
        "valid": *: entry_hole

scalar_committed: Committed scalar cell, focused
    description: The value is persisted (the surgical op is queued/flushed in the background) and the cell keeps focus, caret after the last character. Reached by clicking a committed cell. The cell shows the AUTHORED representation - a bare `species>` in the file stays BARE, never re-derived into `"species>"`; an authored-quoted token opens as its authored text, quotes as characters (no quote mode). A committed token is NOT a dead end - re-editing its text into `key: value` (a QUOTED `"key": value` included) RESTRUCTURES it: the mistyped `species>` recovers into `species>: 12`; the same applies to the omni SELF-VALUE cell and the document root. Typed spaces are spaces - the browser's non-breaking substitutes are normalized before the grammar reads them
    alphanumeric: *: unquoted_scalar_appending
    enter:
        "the text was edited into `key: value` (quoted key allowed) and the key is free in the node": *: scalar_committed
        "the text was edited into a bare `key:` (no value yet) and the key is free": *: value_hole
        "the text reads `key: value` but the key already exists in the node": *: error_flash
        "otherwise": *: entry_hole
    click_outside:
        "the text was edited into `key: value`": *: scalar_committed
        "otherwise": *: scalar_committed
    backspace:
        "caret at the start": *: focus_prev
        "otherwise": *: unquoted_scalar_appending
    tab:
        "the cell is an entry with a previous ordinal sibling": *: scalar_committed
        "otherwise": *: nop
    shift_tab:
        "the cell is an entry below the root level": *: scalar_committed
        "otherwise": *: nop
    left_arrow: *: unquoted_scalar_inserting
    up_arrow: *: focus_prev
    down_arrow: *: focus_next

key_cell_editing: A committed key cell, focused
    description: The entry's KEY as an editable cell - the caret ENTERS it (a click, or an arrow walk in visual order), the text edits in place. Reached by clicking a committed key or arrowing into it. Commit (Enter / blur) RENAMES the key: the server routes by STORAGE - a directory-backed member is renamed on disk (inbound `*`/`~` pointers rewritten), an inline keyed entry has its key token rewritten in the body, value and comments kept (THE CONCRETE IS NOT A STATE). The rename is OPTIMISTIC - the key updates in place and the caret stays in the cell, the persist runs after any pending body edits flush. A key that duplicates a sibling is rejected with the error ring, the typed text kept (keys are unique per node); an empty/unchanged key is a no-op. A DERIVED-anchor key (a positional dir-member's storage provenance) is not editable here
    alphanumeric: *: key_cell_editing
    enter:
        "the new key duplicates a sibling in the node": *: error_flash
        "empty, or unchanged": *: key_cell_editing
        "a free key - the rename persists (dir move or inline rewrite), caret kept in the key cell": *: key_cell_editing
    click_outside: *: key_cell_editing
    backspace:
        "caret at the start": *: focus_prev
        "otherwise": *: key_cell_editing
    up_arrow: *: focus_prev
    down_arrow: *: focus_next

pointer_entry: Reference being entered (a `*`-led hole)
    description: |
        The hole's text begins with `*` - the reference decision (keys.ts classifies it; the
        hole cursor carries the raw, the document is untouched: incompleteness lives in the
        cursor). The PURE face is the plain hole input accumulating the raw; commit
        parses-or-refuses - `*` alone and non-pointers RING and the text stands, nothing is
        ever lost. The SERVER host overlays the SHARED QUERY-CELL editor on the SAME hole
        cursor (the cell registry's holePick seam; cells, dropdown, scope ladder, TOC
        filtering and TOC-click insertion are QUERY_EDITOR.yo's cell_editing in PICK mode):
        candidates come from the HOLDER via GET /api/query, `:` typed in the empty first
        cell CLIMBS the ladder (`*` -> `*:` -> `*::` -> `*:::`), the TOC swaps to the live
        filter (claiming the shared session). A pointer has no SELF-VALUE form (the parser
        refuses a top-level pointer), so the omni diversion never applies: a BARE pointer
        lands as the KEYLESS member it is (`*x` ≡ `- *x` - the chapter pointer-array case);
        only the `k:` ⏎ descend's EMPTY container takes it wholesale (`k: *x`).
    alphanumeric: *: pointer_entry
    colon: *: pointer_entry
    toc_click: *: pointer_entry
    enter:
        "the query REDUCED to a match, or the text parses as a pointer - it commits as the entry's ref VALUE (dangling allowed; hints are never validators): SPACED canonical raw in the IR, COMPACT on the wire (yed-sync payloadOf). A reference holds no children, so the next SIBLING hole opens - the one exception to the level rule": *: entry_hole
        "the text is not a pointer the wire can carry": *: error_flash
    backspace:
        "the star deleted natively - the plain empty hole returns": *: entry_hole
        "otherwise": *: pointer_entry

pointer_pick_editing: Reference retarget (the `pick` cursor)
    description: |
        Enter on a committed atom (atom_focused) opened its RAW for editing - the pick
        cursor (state.ts): the pointer STAYS in the document while its spaced display raw
        edits behind the projected `*` (no remove+insert churn against the sync flush). The
        PURE face is a plain input over the raw; the SERVER host mounts the query kit
        seeded from it (pointer-spell pointerCells). Commit parses-or-refuses; an UNCHANGED
        raw commits as a no-op; leaving with arrows commits too - garbage refuses the move.
        The server kit's blur ABANDONS (ctx.pick.cancel - back onto the atom, the document
        untouched); its Escape and eviction commit (QUERY_EDITOR.yo semantics).
    alphanumeric: *: pointer_pick_editing
    colon: *: pointer_pick_editing
    toc_click: *: pointer_pick_editing
    enter:
        "the raw parses (or stands unchanged) - the retarget lands and Enter walks the SIBLING rule": *: entry_hole
        "not a pointer the wire can carry": *: error_flash
    backspace:
        "the emptied raw - the REFERENCE goes, the entry's name survives as the named hole (removeLevel)": *: entry_hole
        "at the start of a non-empty raw": *: focus_prev
        "otherwise": *: pointer_pick_editing
    tab: "block rows: indent/dedent (refused on a committed row, visibly); flow: the walk"
    up_arrow: *: focus_prev
    down_arrow: *: focus_next

block_scalar_editing: Block scalar editing
    description: The AUTHORED header (`|`, `|-`, `|+`, `>`, ...) is PROJECTED verbatim from the concrete and KEPT - commits re-emit the same header over the edited lines (a fresh block typed via `|` is authored `|`). The text lines edit below it exactly as they sit in the source, indented, in the ordinary editor styling - not a foreign-looking box. BIRTH is the header + ENTER (the whole header can be typed first - `|-`, `>-`; a digit indicator refuses, visibly): a valid "" scalar materializes per the hole's shape (named / ordinal / the empty container's whole value / the omni self line at its row; FLOW refuses - the flow grammar owns Enter) and the textarea opens with the body pending in the cursor. A block SELF-VALUE line edits the same way. Enter is a NEWLINE here, never a commit; leaving from an edge line (arrows) commits the body
    alphanumeric: *: block_scalar_editing
    enter: *: block_scalar_editing
    ctrl_enter: *: entry_hole
    tab: *: entry_hole
    shift_tab: *: entry_hole
    click_outside: *: scalar_committed
    up_arrow:
        "caret on the first line": *: focus_prev
        "otherwise": *: block_scalar_editing
    down_arrow:
        "caret on the last line": *: focus_next
        "otherwise": *: block_scalar_editing

grammar_mirror: The executable grammar
    description: Since the yed-redesign, WHAT A KEY MEANS lives in ONE pure table - dispatch.ts interpret(key, site), tested transition by transition in yed-dispatch.test.ts. The flow states below DESCRIBE that table for a human reader; the table is normative. A grammar change edits dispatch.ts, its test, and this file together. New since the redesign - Backspace in an EMPTIED key cell un-names the pair (one press, one level), and Enter at the gap past a closer opens a sibling in a BLOCK container too

flow_map_editing: Flow mapping editing
    description: The `{` and `}` are projected with entry cells between them, comma-separated; the cursor sits in the current inner cell, which follows the entry-hole grammar (`key colon space` expected). THE BRACKET IS AUTHORED - `{}` and `[]` are different values, so a `{` stays a `{` while its first key is still coming (`{12` is on its way to `{12: 3}`, and `{` `{` to `{{}: 12}` - a flow token used as a KEY). Only the shape the file cannot hold overrides it: a decided KEYED entry wears the map brackets, since `[k: v]` does not parse. A pair in progress is DRAWN but never WRITTEN - no op reaches the server until the key lands. ONE PRESS, ONE LEVEL - collapsing a structure returns the ENTRY that held it to what it was before it was typed: inside a flow token a keyless entry was decided BY the bracket alone, so it goes back to undecided, while in a block container the `- ` is its own decision and stays
    key_colon_space: *: value_hole
    close_brace: *: scalar_committed
    comma: *: flow_map_editing
    enter:
        "the token cannot be spread (a keyed+keyless mixture)": *: scalar_committed
        "otherwise": *: flow_map_spread
    backspace:
        "the inner cell is empty and it is the only one": *: empty_cell_of_origin
        "otherwise": *: flow_map_editing

flow_seq_editing: Flow sequence editing
    description: The `[` and `]` are projected with value cells between them, comma-separated; the cursor sits in the current inner cell. A sequence has NO keys, so a `:` past a closer inside one is refused (in a flow MAP it makes the token that entry's key). EVERY CELL IS WALKABLE - a token is one line of several cells, so the left/right arrows CROSS them at a cell edge, including into the KEY cells, which are editable like any other. A token opened INSIDE a spread one is spread too, since json5p expands everything under the switch
    alphanumeric: *: unquoted_scalar_appending
    close_bracket: *: scalar_committed
    comma: *: flow_seq_editing
    enter:
        "the token cannot be spread (a keyed+keyless mixture)": *: scalar_committed
        "otherwise": *: flow_seq_spread
    backspace:
        "the inner cell is empty and it is the only one": *: empty_cell_of_origin
        "otherwise": *: flow_seq_editing

flow_map_spread: Flow mapping spread to K&R
    description: THE SPREAD IS A PROPERTY OF THE WHOLE TOKEN, never of a level inside it: a nested `{a: [1]}` is ONE token (json5p expands everything under the switch), so Enter anywhere in it spreads from the OUTERMOST bracket and Backspace past any closer joins all of it. NO TRAPS AT THE EDGES - ENTER NEVER CLOSES a token (the closer does): on an empty cell it allocates the row and the caret STAYS inside, because a legal construct like `[12]` must never be a place where editing stops. An untyped element is dropped rather than written as `""`. PAST THE CLOSER Backspace STEPS BACK INSIDE, onto the token's last inner cell - it does NOT join (at document scale that reflowed a whole file under someone who was deleting, and asking for a container entry's own cell focused nothing at all, which was a lock). THE JOIN is the text editor's gesture instead: Backspace at the HEAD OF THE TOKEN'S FIRST LINE - every level from the outermost bracket down being element 0 - pulls that line up onto the opener's and collapses the whole token. Just past the closer Backspace always has somewhere to go - it re-opens a cell inside an EMPTY token, joins a spread one, or steps onto the last cell. THE NEWLINE GESTURE. A comma keeps the next pair on this line; ENTER puts it on the next one, which SPREADS the token - one pair per row, the closer alone on the last. On disk that is an inline concrete switch to json5p (CONCRETES.md Collection style), so it is written by json5p's own serializer. The cells and the comma/closer grammar are unchanged; only the layout differs. A container json5p cannot hold - a keyed+keyless MIXTURE - does not spread, and the pair simply opens inline. Backspace just past the closer JOINS the token back onto one line (the caret stays in the after-cell)
    key_colon_space: *: value_hole
    close_brace: *: scalar_committed
    comma: *: flow_map_spread
    enter:
        "otherwise": *: flow_map_spread
    backspace:
        "the inner cell is empty and it is the only one": *: empty_cell_of_origin
        "the caret is past the closer": *: flow_map_editing
        "otherwise": *: flow_map_spread

flow_seq_spread: Flow sequence spread to K&R
    description: The sequence half of `flow_map_spread` - Enter puts the next element on its own row (the json5p concrete switch), a comma keeps it on this one, and Backspace past the closer joins the token back
    alphanumeric: *: unquoted_scalar_appending
    close_bracket: *: scalar_committed
    comma: *: flow_seq_spread
    enter:
        "otherwise": *: flow_seq_spread
    backspace:
        "the inner cell is empty and it is the only one": *: empty_cell_of_origin
        "the caret is past the closer": *: flow_seq_editing
        "otherwise": *: flow_seq_spread

entry_hole: Fresh entry hole
    description: An empty row without a marker yet - the typed prefix decides the row's shape, exactly as a line of yamlover source would. A BARE scalar token committed here becomes the NODE's own scalar line (the omni self-value, entered as-is, no marker; at most one per block). An array element is spelled with its `- ` marker, a field with its key and colon. Reached after every entry-level commit, so typing flows row to row without reaching for the mouse
    alphanumeric: *: unquoted_scalar_appending
    dash_space: *: value_hole
    key_colon_space:
        "the key already exists in the node": *: error_flash
        "otherwise": *: value_hole
    key_colon_enter:
        "the key already exists in the node": *: error_flash
        "otherwise": *: entry_hole
    bang_bang_lt: *: meta_tag_editing
    asterisk: *: pointer_entry
    pipe: *: block_scalar_editing
    open_brace: *: flow_map_editing
    open_bracket: *: flow_seq_editing
    enter: *: nop
    backspace: *: focus_prev
    tab:
        "a previous ordinal sibling exists": *: entry_hole
        "otherwise": *: nop
    shift_tab:
        "the hole is below the root level": *: entry_hole
        "otherwise": *: nop
    up_arrow: *: focus_prev
    down_arrow: *: focus_next

value_hole: Entry value hole
    description: The entry's marker is drawn (`- `, or the key with its colon) and the empty value cell after it holds the cursor. `dash_space` / `key_colon_space` here open the nested structure COMPACTLY - the fresh marker and its value cell stay on THIS row
    alphanumeric: *: unquoted_scalar_appending
    asterisk: *: pointer_entry
    pipe: *: block_scalar_editing
    open_brace: *: flow_map_editing
    open_bracket: *: flow_seq_editing
    dash_space: *: value_hole
    key_colon_space:
        "the key already exists in the node": *: error_flash
        "otherwise": *: value_hole
    key_colon_enter: *: entry_hole
    enter: *: entry_hole
    backspace:
        "an uncommitted key - the colon is undone, its text (quoted spelling kept) returns to the hole": *: unquoted_scalar_appending
        "an uncommitted dash - the marker is undone": *: entry_hole
        "otherwise": *: focus_prev
    tab:
        "a previous ordinal sibling exists": *: value_hole
        "otherwise": *: nop
    shift_tab:
        "the entry is below the root level": *: value_hole
        "otherwise": *: nop
    up_arrow: *: focus_prev
    down_arrow: *: focus_next

meta_tag_editing: Meta tag editing
    description: The `!!<` and `>` are projected with the tag-content cell between them, cursor inside
    alphanumeric: *: meta_tag_editing
    gt: *: value_hole
    enter: *: value_hole
    backspace:
        "the content became empty": *: empty_cell_of_origin
        "otherwise": *: meta_tag_editing

empty_cell_of_origin: Back to the hole this cell grew from
    description: A pseudo-state - the projected structure (sigil, braces, marker) is dismantled and the cell returns to the hole it was born from; the document root returns to the `empty` state, an entry returns to `entry_hole`

atom_focused: The caret stands ON an atom (a `*` pointer)
    description: |
        The atom is a single non-text cell (dispatch.ts `cell: "atom"`) - the caret stands ON it,
        never inside it. It walks, it deletes, it copies; typing into it rings - Enter opens the
        reference for editing (pointer_pick_editing). An OPAQUE atom (a blob) has no raw to
        edit, so its Enter refuses, visibly.
    backspace: *: entry_hole            # removeLevel - the value goes, a name survives as the named hole
    comma:
        "inside a flow container": *: entry_hole   # nextElement - the next element's hole
        "in block rows": *: atom_focused           # no meaning - falls through, nothing
    closer:
        "the matching closer, in flow": *: gap_after_token   # closeToken
        "the wrong closer": *: atom_focused                  # refuse - the ring
    enter:
        "a pointer atom": *: pointer_pick_editing  # the pick intent - the raw opens for editing
        "an opaque atom (a blob)": *: atom_focused # refuse - nothing to edit, visibly
    alphanumeric: *: atom_focused       # refuse - typing into an atom RINGS
    arrows: "the neighbouring position per the walk order"

# --- The CHAPTER projection ---------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The SAME document, drawn as prose/headings/sections/lists. The machine is PURE and lives in
# tools/yed/src/chapter/ (site.ts -> dispatch.ts -> apply.ts, watchdog.ts enforcing the
# never-locked law); tools/yed/test/chapter-dispatch.test.ts mirrors THIS section as data.
# The legacy implementation (chapter-editor/view.tsx) runs the same states until the port flips.
# The typing grammar above does not apply here (no holes, no `- `/`key:`) - EXCEPT inside a
# SOURCE chunk (a structure with an explicit non-chapter `!!<...>` type), where the chapter
# grammar DECLINES every key and the source grammar above owns the caret entirely.
# THE PROSE EXCEPTION governs Enter; THE FORMAT RULE governs Tab and the format commands.
# Ctrl+Enter is THE TABLE GESTURE: outside a table it INITIATES one (the bar's table button
# as a keystroke - the format command's gates apply), inside one it appends a ROW.
# Closed intents: splitProse joinPrev joinNext nest unwrap indent dedent cellWalk appendRow
# enterWalk move role format nop refuse. Re-choosing the ACTIVE format is idle (nop).
# THE FORMAT RULE is ITEM-LOCAL for "normal": ¶ in a list extracts THE ITEM (the list splits
# around it; a single-item list dissolves) - the leaf->list wrap's exact inverse, so the
# format cycle converges and committed labour survives.
# THE ROLES LAW (tools/yed/src/chapter/roles.ts - the one decision table BOTH faces consult;
# the read renderer's chapterFlow and this projection's cells/walk can never disagree, and the
# face-parity gate pins it):
#   - a KEYED entry that is not title/description (a planning field, a keyed-remainder member)
#     is BODY, its key the gutter label (the null key shows as `~`);
#   - the title renders at its AUTHORED slot (`meta.selfAt`), not hoisted first;
#   - the title test is hasSelfValue - ANY scalar self titles the chapter;
#   - a LEGACY keyed `title:` entry is the heading (an editable title line at the entry);
#   - a body-positioned MEMBER is body whatever its storage name (anchorKey is provenance,
#     never a field - a member named `description` stays a chunk);
#   - the annotation overlay keys (`yamlover-annotations`/`-fragments`) are hidden; an
#     annotated scalar chunk stays the prose it is (never a spurious subchapter).

chapter_title_editing: A chapter title / subtitle, focused
    description: The document's (or a subchapter's) self-value as an editable heading, or the keyed `description` as the subtitle - the SAME editor at every level, indented. Title and description are OPTIONAL and have NO placeholder cells - a cell exists only when its model does; any one-line chunk takes (or gives back) the role via the bar's T / D buttons. Opening the editor lands in the first cell that EXISTS - title, description, first paragraph, or an empty chapter's bootstrap paragraph. A title EMPTIED character by character drops its self line and the caret lands on the chapter's first remaining stop (never nowhere - the reported lost focus)
    enter:
        "at the START of a non-empty subchapter title - the word-processor push-down: an empty paragraph opens BEFORE the whole subchapter": *: chapter_prose_editing
        "the first body chunk is a SUBCHAPTER heading or an ATOM - the walk would land on something untypeable, so an empty paragraph opens as the FIRST chunk instead (insertHead)": *: chapter_prose_editing
        "otherwise - the walk: title → description → the body (a first paragraph is made if none)": *: chapter_title_editing
    ctrl_enter: *: format_switched                # THE TABLE GESTURE - the titled block becomes a table (the bar's ▦)
    tab:
        "a title whose PREVIOUS sibling is a chapter AND the subtree can be re-rooted honestly - the whole subchapter nests under it, one level deeper": *: chapter_title_editing
        "the subtree carries NESTED member storage, a blob, or a holder-relative pointer - THE MOVE LAW: the move would copy the projection inline, orphan the directories and rebind the pointers, so it RINGS (a flat member with a clean subtree still moves - the detach)": *: error_flash
        "otherwise (the root title, or a plain-paragraph neighbour) - Tab never conscripts the previous chunk": *: nop
    shift_tab:
        "a SUBCHAPTER title - the title dissolves back into a plain paragraph (the wrap's inverse, zero ops when childless): its body splices out after it in order, a keyed description dissolving into an ordinal chunk to move": *: chapter_prose_editing
        "the root title": *: nop
    ctrl_alt_number: *: format_switched
    role_button: *: chapter_prose_editing         # T / D on the title/description itself UNMAKES it - the text survives as a plain chunk
    up_down_arrow: *: chapter_title_editing       # one line - both walk to the adjacent editable cell in document order
    backspace:
        "at the START of a subchapter title": *: chapter_prose_editing  # joinPrev - THE TITLE DISSOLVES into the preceding paragraph (Tab-wrap's inverse by deletion); a MATERIALIZED subchapter rings instead
        "the root title, or mid-text": *: chapter_title_editing         # native editing

chapter_prose_editing: A prose paragraph, focused
    description: A body paragraph as the shared marklower editor. Enter makes a SIBLING paragraph (THE PROSE EXCEPTION), not a descent. An EMPTY chapter shows one bootstrap paragraph (no entry until the first typed text - merely opening writes nothing) - and the bootstrap is NOT an idle state; a format command MATERIALIZES the first entry of that kind (a one-cell table in its creation flow, a one-item list, an empty paragraph), T makes an empty title to type into, D an empty description
    enter: *: chapter_prose_editing               # split at the caret; the tail is a new sibling paragraph
    ctrl_enter:
        "outside a table - THE TABLE GESTURE: the paragraph wraps as a one-cell table in its creation flow (Tab grows columns, Ctrl+Enter then appends rows); on the bootstrap paragraph the table MATERIALIZES as the first entry": *: format_switched
        "inside a table cell's inner chunks": *: table_cell_editing  # appendRow - the gesture stays one gesture
    tab:
        "any paragraph NESTS - it becomes the sole paragraph of a fresh UNTITLED group (`- x` -> `- - x`, one replace op), STAYING a paragraph one level deeper. The title role is EXPLICIT: T titles the group (and the group materializes in a dir chapter once it is titled with body). The group carries the session wrap badge": *: chapter_prose_editing
    shift_tab:
        "below the root level": *: chapter_prose_editing        # lift it out of its subchapter
        "otherwise": *: nop
    backspace:
        "the caret is at the start": *: chapter_prose_editing   # joinPrev - WALK-ADJACENT: into the previous walk cell (a paragraph merges; a subchapter TITLE dissolves in title-text-first, the chapter's OWN heading included - the chapter flattens around the caret; a MATERIALIZED subchapter rings); description/table/atom partners RING
        "otherwise": *: chapter_prose_editing                   # native contentEditable editing
    delete:
        "the caret is at the end": *: chapter_prose_editing     # joinNext - WALK-ADJACENT: pulls the next walk cell in (a following subchapter's TITLE dissolves, its body splicing out; an emptied untitled group husk-removes)
        "otherwise": *: chapter_prose_editing                   # native
    ctrl_alt_number: *: format_switched
    role_button: *: chapter_title_editing         # T / D makes THIS chunk the chapter's title / description (only offered where the chapter lacks one)
    up_down_arrow: *: chapter_prose_editing       # at the first/last visual line, the caret walks to the adjacent cell

list_item_editing: A typographical-list item, focused
    description: An item of a tagged bullets/numbered list. Tab nests it as a SUBLIST of the same kind - the same model move as a subchapter, since an untagged nested container inherits the list's kind
    enter: *: list_item_editing
    ctrl_enter: *: format_switched                # THE TABLE GESTURE - the list retags as a table
    tab:
        "a previous item exists": *: list_item_editing
        "otherwise": *: nop
    shift_tab: *: list_item_editing
    ctrl_alt_number: *: format_switched

format_switched: The block's format was chosen (bar / Ctrl+Alt+1..4)
    description: A pseudo-state - the block the caret is in is retagged (→ chapter DROPS the tag, → bullets/numbered/table retag or wrap a leaf), re-projected in place, and the caret stays in its content. The target is the ENCLOSING block: standing in a list item retags the list, in a table cell the table

table_cell_editing: A table cell, focused (the spreadsheet flow)
    description: A cell hosts the NORMAL chapter flow (row-cell folds back to chapter rules) - Enter splits it into chunks. The FIRST row defines the width (Tab grows columns while the table has one row); a second row fixes the column count
    enter:
        "plain": *: chapter_prose_editing           # splitProse - the cell becomes a container of chunks, the caret in the tail; a SCALAR row wraps to its one cell FIRST (its entries are cells - splitting it directly would mint a phantom column); a join that reduces the chunks back to ONE paragraph FOLDS the cell back to its scalar (the exact inverse - no one-item husk)
        "ctrl": *: table_cell_editing               # appendRow - a fresh row of the table's width, from anywhere IN the table (a cell, or a split cell's inner chunks)
    backspace:
        "mid-text": *: table_cell_editing           # native char delete
        "at a cell's start": *: table_cell_editing  # THE TABLE UNWIND LADDER - the caret steps into the previous cell and deletion continues there (walking out of the table past its first cell)
        "at the first position of an ALL-EMPTY row": *: table_cell_editing  # deleteRow - the row leaves the table; the LAST row's departure removes the emptied table itself (the husk), caret on the stop before it
    tab:
        "mid-row": *: table_cell_editing            # cellWalk +1 - the next cell
        "at a row's end": *: table_cell_editing     # cellWalk +1 wraps to the next row's first cell
        "at a SINGLE row's end": *: table_cell_editing  # appendColumn - the creation flow grows the width
        "at the VERY last cell": *: table_cell_editing  # appendRow - the width is fixed past row one
    shift_tab:
        "not the first cell": *: table_cell_editing # cellWalk -1
        "the first cell": *: nop                    # never un-appends
    up_down_arrow:
        "an adjacent row exists": *: table_cell_editing   # a GRID move - the same column, one row up/down
        "the top/bottom edge": *: chapter_prose_editing   # the caret LEAVES the table; past the DOCUMENT's last stop, ↓ ADDS a fresh chunk right AFTER THE TABLE in the table's own container - a table inside a subchapter grows the subchapter, never the root (and never spamming empties)
    left_right_arrow:
        "at the cell's start/end edge": *: table_cell_editing  # cellWalk - the caret hops to the previous/next cell
        "mid-text": *: table_cell_editing                      # native editing
    plus_buttons: *: table_cell_editing             # + row / + col show while the caret is in the grid; a scalar row becomes its two-cell array on + col

source_chunk_editing: Inside a SOURCE chunk (an explicitly non-chapter-typed structure)
    description: The chunk renders and edits as inline yamlover SOURCE - the standard cells and the typing grammar ABOVE own every key (holes, `- `/`key:`, flow); the chapter grammar declines. Arrows walk across the chunk's boundary into the neighbouring chapter cells. The mode is DERIVED per render - dropping the type reverts the chunk to prose/chapter editing

chapter_atom_focused: A reference / link / binary chunk, focused (one walkable stop)
    description: A RAW pointer chunk hosts the source editor's own reference cells (a nested editor over a one-entry wrapper - the pick machinery, verbatim); links and binaries are read-only stops. THE BOUNDARY HAND-OFF - the wrapper holds exactly ONE atom, so on the IDLE atom every walking key leaves the chunk through the CHAPTER machine instead of ringing at the sub-editor's edge; while PICKING the kit owns every key (dropdown arrows, the cell walk)
    enter:
        "a pointer atom": *: pointer_pick_editing   # the reference opens for retargeting (the source grammar's pick)
        "a link / binary": *: chapter_atom_focused  # nothing to edit - swallowed
    backspace:
        "a pointer atom - the emptied reference DISSOLVES the chunk into an empty paragraph, caret in it": *: chapter_prose_editing
        "a link / binary": *: chapter_atom_focused  # the chapter grammar leaves it standing
    up_down_arrow: *: chapter_prose_editing         # the walk continues past the atom (whatever cell is adjacent)
    left_right_arrow: *: chapter_prose_editing      # an atom is ONE stop - any arrow leaves it
    tab: *: nop                                     # swallowed, never a browser focus walk
    alphanumeric: *: chapter_atom_focused           # typing into an atom does nothing here
